n look at what happens when Julie  goes on her first date with Anthony  suddenly the camera is locked down in  position and it basically stays this way  for much of the rest of the film it's a  beautiful evocative way of communicating  that this relationship has caused a  shift in Julie's life and you might  think that locking a camera down onto a  tripod might limit the expressiveness  but you can do so much just through  composition look at this scene where  there's a pivotal Revelation about the  relationship which I won't spoil  see how this conversation is framed in a  fairly standard way and then look at how  suddenly it goes off kilter and then  crosses the 180 degree Rule and switches  to a much more distinct framing that  separates Julie over here and almost  causes this dissociative effect in the  conversation it looks as if she's  separated from what's Happening almost  observing the conversation from a  distance and then there's this other  beautiful subtle moment watch as Anthony  returns to the table and his shadow  briefly passes over Julie's face it's  like a literal representation of the  shadow that this moment is casting on  her  Reuben oslin's Force measure is another  great example of doing a lot with a  fairly minimalistic approach to  cinematography  the film opens with this family on a ski  vacation and they're all having a pretty  good happy time and we get these shots  where all of them are included together  in the frame but then watch what happens  to the compositions after there's an  incident that causes a rift between the  husband and wife through a combination  of composition and blocking we begin to  see how things are literally fracturing  and a divide is created between the  husband and wife there's a great  sequence where another couple comes to  visit this husband and wife and the  husband and wife talk about their  argument which literally causes a rift  then between the visiting couple the  composition and blocking of this scene  illustrates how that's happening  perfectly  this is one movie that had the  misfortune of getting an American remake  and one of the things that the English  language version does besides just suck  a lot of the subtlety out of the writing  is remove this element of composition  and cinematography and entirely from how  the story is conveyed and the American  version is just so much more flat and  empty because of it one director whose  career I think is an excellent  illustration of kind of the range of  what can constitute great cinematography  is Paul Thomas Anderson when you say  Paul Thomas Anderson in cinematography  you probably immediately think of his  oscar-winning work with Robert elswit on  There Will Be Blood or the really  energetic evocative work of Boogie  Nights or the long tracking shots we  find in Magnolia those movies have  incredible cinematography but just as  impressive to me is a lot of his later  work like inherent Vice and Phantom  thread in these films there aren't these  iconic images or moments that stick in  my mind but what is just as impactful is  the way these movies feel Phantom thread  isn't one of these movies that comes at  you with these striking images instead  it's more of a soft mood and vibe that  just kind of saturates the entire film  and that's not an accident at all it's  very deliberate and constructed the  subdued colors and the softness of the  highlights are created by the choice of  film stock and the filters placed in  front of the lens there's a BTS video  that shows some of the test footage they  shot  [Applause]  and you can see how much work went into  just choosing the right filters for the  lens and film stock for this film and  those elements match tonally with the  performances and story that the film is  telling these are the smaller details of  cinematography that don't usually get  talked about outside of the more  technical discussions but these choices  can have a huge impact on how the final  film feels PTA does the same thing in  the master inherent Vice and licorice  pizza each of these movies have their  own look that is distinct from the other  but which feels completely appropriate  to the story each film is telling of  course what constitutes subtle is  culturally and personally subjective I  kind of doubt that masaki kobayashi's  harakiri was perceived as subtle when it  was released in 1962 but when you  compare it to some of the stuff that  we're used to now  it's essentially a master class in  Restraint there's a lot of bold striking  images in this film but what I want to  focus on is not just movies that have  subtle cinematography overall but how  subtler elements of cinematography that  we're less likely to notice can still  convey meaning one of the ways Kobayashi  does that in this film is by changing  the cinematographic style based on what  he's trying to represent when he's  establishing this very traditional  orderly and almost tyrannical Samurai  Clan at the beginning of the film he  uses very precise compositions that have  very little movement but then when he's  introducing a character that is  challenging some of that tradition he  uses camera movements that at that time  would have been more bold and  challenging to traditions of filmmaking  these shifts in stylistic approach to  convey meaning is one of my favorite  tools that cinematographers can use and  I think it's one of the subtler ways  that profound meaning can be  communicated in a way that might kind of  fly under the radar for viewers at least  on a first watch sometimes the shift  isn't in the stylistic approach so much  as the perspective from which things are  shot in my first ever video for this  channel I talked about how in  Nightcrawler director Dan Gilroy and  cinematographer Robert Ellsworth shift  the perspective over the course of the  film from one where we're watching Luke  Bloom from a detached perspective film  these crime scenes and disasters in a  preda